Subject: dwrite: rasterise ClearType runs at subpixel resolution

A glyph run analysis that asks for DWRITE_TEXTURE_CLEARTYPE_3x1 gets a
texture three times as wide, one sample per subpixel, and that is what
DirectWrite's horizontal resolution comes from. Wine fills it by
rendering the glyph once in greyscale and copying each coverage value
into all three subpixels: font.c had

    dst[3*x] = dst[3*x+1] = dst[3*x+2] = src[x] | dst[3*x];

so the texture had the shape of ClearType and none of its resolution.
Text drawn through DirectWrite resolves horizontally at one sample per
pixel where Windows uses three, which is the largest single difference
between the two in ordinary interface text.

Render the outline scaled three times horizontally, one coverage sample
per subpixel, and filter each row with the same five-tap FIR FreeType
uses for FT_RENDER_MODE_LCD, which spreads a sample over its neighbours
so a stem landing on one channel averages back to grey. The outline is
scaled rather than passed through FT_Render_Glyph because the caller
supplies the destination bitmap and its exact bounds.

The rendered form is now part of the glyph cache key: a greyscale and a
ClearType bitmap for the same glyph and size differ in both content and
length, and sharing an entry between them would return one where the
other was asked for.

This changes what dwrite produces, not what its callers do with it.
d2d1 currently wraps the texture in an alpha mask three times as wide
and samples it back down, which averages the three samples into one
again, so Direct2D text does not benefit until that path blends per
channel. GDI interop, which consumes the texture directly, does.

